% ============================================================================
% weathering-cause-type.query.adj — worked recall over the earth-science
% weathering-cause-type library.
% ============================================================================
% The shape a learner (or an LLM) produces to ANSWER "is heating and cooling
% a physical or chemical cause of weathering?" (direct binding) or "which
% causes are physical weathering?" (reverse, one-to-many binding) — it
% states no earth-science fact of its own, it only `import`s the grounded
% table and asks binding queries. The third and fourth queries run the
% relation BACKWARD as a genuine one-to-many recall (three physical causes,
% two chemical causes). The final query demonstrates honest abstention:
% `erosion` is a real Earth process the table's own cited article discusses
% at length, but the article's own structure treats erosion as a distinct,
% LATER process that moves weathering's products away — not a third
% weathering type — so recalling it abstains rather than inventing one.
% ============================================================================

import "weathering-cause-type.adj"

? weathering_cause_type(heating_and_cooling, $Type)   % expect physical (direct)
? weathering_cause_type(acid_exposure, $Type)         % expect chemical (direct)
? weathering_cause_type($C, physical)                 % expect heating_and_cooling ; foreign_crystal_growth ; rock_collision (reverse, one-to-many)
? weathering_cause_type($C, chemical)                 % expect acid_exposure ; oxygen_exposure (reverse, one-to-many)
? weathering_cause_type(erosion, $Type)                % expect abstain -- erosion is a distinct, later process, not a weathering type
